{"id": "sdqa0060", "pool": "sdqa", "tier": "always", "audio_s": 3.43, "n_chunks": 4, "n_concurrent": 4, "gen_active_at_eot": true, "eot_score": 0.2944, "eot_read_ms": 32, "reference_answer": "a class of organophosphorus compounds with the general structure O=P(OR)3", "query": "What is an organophosphate?", "transcript": "What is an organophosphate?", "mode": "escalated", "relay": "An organophosphate is a chemical compound that contains a phosphate group attached to an organic molecule. They're often used in pesticides and some industrial applications, but many can be toxic because they interfere with important enzymes in the body.", "expert_answer": "An **organophosphate** is an organic chemical compound that contains a **phosphate ester** group—i.e., it is derived from phosphoric acid with carbon-containing groups attached.\n\nThey are commonly used in **insecticides**, **nerve agents**, and some industrial chemicals. Many organophosphates are toxic because they can inhibit **acetylcholinesterase**, an enzyme needed for normal nerve signal regulation.", "expert_latency_s": 2.76, "stall_ms": 36, "relay_ms": 1625}
